I think Scotland will become independent  my view is that that is the direction of  travel but on the question of timing for  the last 12 months I've been seeing very  clearly that I don't think it's right to  consider that decision where things are  so unclear and uncertain around breaks  it so as First Minister I won't give  consideration to the timing until we've  got some breaks at clarity the message I  was given to my party at our conference  yesterday is that that gives us an  opportunity not to worry all the time  about when we might vote again on  Independence but instead to engage in  the substance of the arguments and to  address people in Scotland who still ask  why we should be independent and I think  that debate is timely because there's  going to be change breaks it makes that  inevitable most people think breaks that  will meet the country tutor so this is  an opportunity to look at whether  there's a better alternative for  Scotland and focus on hope and optimism  and how we maximize our potential as a  country so that's the opportunity I was  encouraging my party to grasp but when I  spoke to them yesterday so pretty clear  then that you say that there needs to be  a bit more bricks that clarity until we  get more clarity about a referendum on  Scotland so let's talk about brexit  shall we because in 2016 after that  referendum you said that you were  looking at ways to keep Scotland in the  single market even if the UK leave so  how is that going well we are exploring  all options to protect Scotland's  interests I also said in 2016 and I  still see this today that the best  option for the whole of the UK if it's  leaving the EU which I regret is to stay  in the single market and the customs  union and I happen to think that there  is still a real prospect of securing  that it might not feel like that just  now when the Prime Minister is always  ruling it out but we only have to look  at the debated cabinet I do yes and  we'll see how the votes cool in the  House of Commons this week but even  beyond that I do think that is still a  reasonable prospect because the Prime  Minister's position is I think just  undeliverable and unsustainable she's  trying to reconcile all sorts of things  that are Eric and sale  and the common-sense solution as well as  I guess the best Democratic compromise  given the divisions of opinion around  BRICS is to stay in the single market  and the customs union so I don't think  there is any reason to give up on that  argument right now well that seems  remarkably optimistic given that both  the Conservative Party and labour are  saying that they are not looking for a  single market membership do you think  that something could be on the cards  like what is happening with the Irish  situation and the EU said that they're  looking into a bespoke agreement to  solve the problem of the border in  Northern Ireland is that something that  you could envisage happening for  Scotland a bespoke solution well one of  the reasons why I don't think we should  give up on customs union and single  market membership is that that is the  simplest way to resolve the issues in  Northern Ireland and resolve the issues  around the border between the north and  the Republic of Ireland so you know it's  not an ideal scenario but I'm just  trying to get to you know the reality of  the situation could you be arguing for  something bespoke for Scotland well if  there is a sport arrangement for  Northern Ireland and again I've said  this all along that keeps the Northern  Ireland in or effectively in the single  market then I do think it becomes all  the more important that those options  are open to Scotland because if we find  ourselves outside the single market  which would be damaging and a whole host  of ways anyway but Northern Ireland is  still and then Scotland would find  itself economically at a competitive  disadvantage and that would have  implications for jobs and investments in  Scotland so I think it stands to reason  that is First Minister I would be  arguing for similar options for Scotland  but I come back to my original point I  think it's right for the whole of the UK  to remain in the single market and the  customs union jobs depend on that  investment depend on that living  standards depend on that and I don't  think we should simply accept the  meltdown that is coming to use the  Foreign Secretary's language if the  Prime Minister and the government  continue to argue a position that is not  deliverable you know treason is spending  all of our time trying to keep our  cabinet together talking about options  that unite them I'm not sure whether  she's going to succeed even in that but  the options there top  have already effectively been ruled out  by the EU we need to see some realism  emerge in these discussions and that's  why I do think we've seen labour shift I  think on the customs union I think we  may see them shift more on single market  membership I think this is a moment for  those of us who believe in these  outcomes to continue to argue the case  for them  you're very critical that about Tereza  Mazar stance do you have a look at the  other people around the cabinet table  and feel a degree of relief that it's  her doing the negotiations rather than  for example Boris Johnson or David Davis  well yeah I mean I don't think Boris  Johnson should be anywhere near a  government office you know he must be at  one of the least fit people to hold a  high office of state that we've ever  seen and I thought that before his  comments at the end of last week he's  entirely playing to a heart breaks a  tear gallery it sounds as if Boris  Johnson fights it sounds as if none of  the members of the Thule cabin actually  care about jobs and living standards and  prospects for people the length and  breadth of the UK and that's got to  change and if the cabinet the Prime  Minister are not prepared to change and  argue for a more realistic and sensible  position then I hope we see the House of  Commons forced them into that position  now well we see what happens this week  I'm not seeing him I'm holding my breath  or being overly optimistic but sooner or  later there has to be an outbreak of  common sense and I hope we see that  sooner rather than later I'm talking  about those comments that Boris Johnson  made her one of them was that Donald  Trump might make a better job of the  brexit negotiations he of course has  been ripping up every piece of  diplomatic protocol at the g7 will you  be welcoming him to Scotland when it  comes to visit well we don't know his  plans yet you know I think everybody  knows that I don't see eye to eye with  Donald Trump on very many things of  course if the President of the United  States comes to Scotland and there's an  opportunity to engage with them as first  minister of course I will do that but  you know how many very clear the values  and principles that are important to me  to my government I think too many people  across Scotland and I think in this  uncertain what would we live in right  now it's important to speak up for the  progressive principles those values of  social democracy and I think the more  politicians we hear doing that the  better now before you go I've got to ask  you the World Cup is starting next week  unfortunately Scotland not playing a  part in it so are you gonna be cheering  for jokes  I wish include well in the World Cup  maybe if they win this world cup we can  stop talking about 1966 at long last but  as I was seen in another interview at  the end of the week I drew Iceland in my  office sweepstakes so I'll be keeping an  eye out on their scores as well  okay very diplomatic Leon Verdi Nicola  Sturgeon thanks very much

